{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md", "content": "# Compact-output reassessment after incorrect submissions

When inputs are partitioned by repeated separator rows or columns but demos produce much smaller outputs, dimension ratios help identify possible structure but do not establish the encoding. Keep distinct hypotheses for (a) summarizing interior regions, (b) recording separator/boundary markers at their intersections, and (c) selecting only rows or columns with special markers. For each demo, write down the exact partition counts and map candidate input coordinates or blocks to each output cell, including ordering. Reproduce every output exactly before transferring the map.

After an incorrect compact-output submission, do not merely alter colors or copy another visually salient set of markers. Reopen the task class and output representation, and re-derive dimensions and correspondence from all demos. Repeated, heterogeneous demos may share only an abstract structure; spend another demo only when two explicit mappings predict a materially different discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u093/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/compact-output-reassessment-after-errors.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/stream-expansion-and-failed-stamp.md", "content": "# Stream expansion demos and representation checks

In a task with a 5×10 test, the first two demos had 1D inputs and expanded outputs: 9×1 → 27×2, with each input value repeated across each output row and the sequence arranged as reversed/original/reversed; 1×15 → 3×30, with three identical rows and a sequence arranged as reversed/original. These establish that 1D-to-2D expansion depends on orientation, but do not establish a mapping for a genuinely 2D test.

A speculative answer incorrectly treated the 5×10 test as a 3×2 stamp replication over groups of three rows. The key failure was unsupported transfer of a construction despite the test not matching either demonstrated input class. After the incorrect response, the correct next step is to reopen output representation and seek evidence that distinguishes candidate 2D mappings, rather than cosmetically modifying the stamp. For demos, articulate what the new pair would disambiguate before paying the request cost; requests should resolve a concrete uncertainty.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u093/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/stream-expansion-and-failed-stamp.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/speculative-submission-after-demo.md", "content": "# Avoiding speculative submission after a demo

A returned demonstration pair is evidence to analyze, not permission to extrapolate from a salient resemblance. Record the exact changed and unchanged cells, then check whether the rule is uniquely supported for the current test. If an assumed stamp, anchor, orientation, or source-retention rule is not established by the pair, do not submit a guessed grid. Name at least two plausible hypotheses and request another pair only if their predictions differ in a way that can change the test answer. Analyze every newly returned pair before acting again. This is especially important after a failed submission: changing only one guessed coordinate or preserving a different portion of the candidate is not meaningful recovery unless new evidence supports it.
